INT. HOTEL - 9TH FLOOR CORRIDOR - NIGHT

Long. Carpeted. Quiet as a held breath. Brass numbers glow on doors. A housekeeping cart sits abandoned by the elevators, a room-service tray on the floor, a single shoe in the hall.

CELIA VANCE, 30s, evening dress, heels in hand, walks fast with a phone to her ear. Behind her, a STRANGER in a grey suit follows at a polite distance.

CELIA
(into phone)
Dad I told you I'm fine, the party was fine, I'm in the hallway now, I'm literally at my door.

DAD (V.O.)
(over phone, warm)
You sound off. Is somebody with you?

She glances back. The STRANGER stops, checks his watch, smiles.

CELIA
No. Nobody.

She reaches her door. Swipes. Red light. Swipes again. Red.

ON PHONE: Front Desk: Your key has expired. Please visit reception.

CELIA
(muttering)
Great. Perfect.

The STRANGER steps closer, hands visible.

STRANGER
Having trouble? I can call down. I'm in 912, just there.

CELIA
I'm fine thank you

STRANGER
Of course. I'm Marcus by the way. Marcus Hale. We met at the bar, I bought you the gin.

Celia freezes. A beat. Her face loosens.

CELIA
Oh my god. Marcus. You're the one who spilled it on my coat.

MARCUS
Guilty. I've been trying to apologise all night. Honestly I followed you because I felt terrible.

She laughs, relieved, the phone drooping at her side. Dad's tiny voice keeps talking.

DAD (V.O.)
Celia? Celia are you there

CELIA
(into phone)
Dad I'll call you back

She hangs up. The corridor settles. Down the hall an elevator DINGS and the doors slide, and out walks a woman in a hotel blazer, name badge reading PRIYANKA, and behind her two men in dark coats who are definitely not guests.

MARCUS
(low, not smiling now)
Don't turn around. Walk with me.

CELIA
What

MARCUS
Your father sold something he shouldn't have. They're here for you. I'm the one who's here to get you out.

The housekeeper sets down her cart. Her hand goes to her pocket.
